            "parents": [
                "1f377a31bd311df8b7a6715a390cb0b293e50956"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/27/81027/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Potin Lai",
                "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                "username": "potinlai"
            },
            "createdOn": 1749644027,
            "author": {
                "name": "Potin Lai",
                "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                "username": "potinlai"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is the CX7 emulating a TMP421?  Or is it a real TMP421?\n\nIs this intended to be temporary or is this the proposed permanent solution?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "It's a TMP421 emulated by CX7.\nI think this will remain until we find a safe solution to make the BE NICs (behind an i2c mux) work with PLDMD, or filter out the BE NICs from PLDMD."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can't we just use PLDM for the front end nic?  I don't think we have the same muxing issue there, do we?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "PLDMD will automatic search all exist EID on system and start sensor polling.\nIt may be difficult to make PLDMD work only with FE NICs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "But the commit in front of this that I just merged said:\n\n```\nThe `backend-nic-driver-bind` service is no longer required as Catalina\nnow queries backend NIC temperature via PLDM. This simplifies the system\noperations and reduces unnecessary overhead.\n```\n\nAm I just confused on pldmtool vs pldmd?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Potin Lai",
                        "email": "potin.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "potinlai"
                    },
                    "message": "Sorry about the confusion, we still use `pldmtool` to query the sensors on BE NICs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Ok.  I'd like to have something figured out here, especially for the FE NICs.  We should be able to use pldmd directly on this.\n\nI'll go ahead and merge this now, but I really don't like us having a bunch of \"sensor daemon as shell\" like we're doing now."
                }
            ],
